## 2. Low-Latency Dictation & Delivery Invariants
* **Zero-Block Streaming:** Never introduce synchronous heavy LLM calls into the primary dictation path. Speech cleanup must rely on deterministic regex heuristics (<0.1ms) to eliminate fillers (*um, uh, er, ah*) and duplicate stutters.
* **Focus Safety Invariant:** Desktop HUDs and overlays must never steal focus from the user's active window (`WS_EX_NOACTIVATE | WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW` on Windows; non-activating layer rules on Wayland).
* **120-Second Recording Cutoff:** Recording threads must enforce a strict 120-second timeout guardrail to eliminate runaway memory or corrupted WAV buffers from stuck physical keys.
* **Silent Push-to-Talk:** Avoid audible chimes on toggle or release; provide unobtrusive visual status notifications only.
* **Wayland Delivery Timing Invariant:** Desktop typing injection via `wtype` must use `-s 20 -d 1` (20ms settle, 1ms inter-key delay). Never use excessive delays (`-s 100 -d 2`) which inject 800ms+ of lag into post-transcription delivery.
* **Audio Hardware Drain Invariant:** Client toggle scripts must include a brief 100ms buffer drain (`sleep 0.10`) before sending `kill -INT` to `pw-record`. This permits PipeWire ALSA ring buffers to complete trailing speech phonemes without human-reaction cutoffs.
* **Multi-Line Text Pipeline Invariant:** Text deduplication and post-polish routines must process multi-line strings line-by-line (`split('\n')`). Never perform naive whitespace tokenisation or global `\s{2,}` collapsing on text containing intentional voice-command line breaks (`"new paragraph"`, `"new line"`).
* **Technical Dictionary Boundary Invariant:** Exact phrase match patterns must not rely on bare `\b` word boundaries for technical vocabularies containing symbols (e.g. `.NET`, `C++`). Always enforce negative lookarounds `(?<!\w)` and `(?!\w)` for alphanumeric terms, falling back to non-whitespace boundaries `(?<!\S)` and `(?!\S)` when matching leading or trailing punctuation.
* **Audio Callback Lock Decoupling Invariant:** Real-time biquad rumble filters must remain inside the audio callback to prevent post-speech release lag and maintain valid VAD energy thresholds. Prevent callback thread lock contention by executing `_biquad_stream_filter.process(indata)` outside `self._lock` before the frame list append.

